.TH QR 1
.SH NAME
qr \- QR code encoder
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qr
[
.B -LMQHna
] [
.B -v
.I ver
] [
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Qr
is a real, structural translation of Plan 9's own
.IR qr (1)
.RB ( sys/src/cmd/qr.c ,
1188 lines - see BUGS for a real, severe bug found in the actual
9front source while this translation was written), compiled to Dis
and run by
.IR emu ,
not a host binary. It lives under
.B p9/cmd
rather than
.BR appl/cmd ;
see
.B p9/dis/README
for how to bind it in.
.PP
.I Qr
reads
.I file
(or standard input) and encodes it as a QR code (ISO/IEC 18004),
writing a short fixed header
.RB ( "k8 0 0 side side " ,
five space-padded fields) followed by one raw byte per module
.RB ( 0
or
.BR 16rff ,
i.e.
.B 255 )
to standard output - a raw one-channel-per-pixel image meant to be
piped into a separate image tool, not a viewable file by itself.
.TP
.B -L -M -Q -H
Error-correction level, lowest to highest redundancy (recovering
roughly 7%, 15%, 25%, and 30% of codewords respectively). Default is
.BR -M .
.TP
.B -v
.I ver
Force a specific QR version (1-40, larger means more modules and more
capacity) instead of letting
.I qr
pick the smallest version that fits the input.
.TP
.B -n
Numeric mode: the input must be digits only.
.TP
.B -a
Alphanumeric mode: the input must be drawn from QR's own restricted
alphanumeric alphabet (digits, upper-case letters, space, and
.BR $%*+-./: ).
.PP
With neither
.B -n
nor
.BR -a ,
the input is encoded in byte mode (any byte value at all).
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/qr.b
(translated from
.BR qr.c ;
see its own header comment for the full derivation, including the
real encode() bug this section's BUGS describes)
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR qrtest (1)
.SH BUGS
.B qr.c
itself - the real, deployed 9front source, not this translation -
has a genuine, severe bug:
.BR encode() 's
own local bit-count cursor
.RB ( bc )
is used before ever being initialized, which is undefined behaviour
in C. Confirmed by building the real source and decoding its own
output with a real, independent QR decoder: every QR code version 2
and above came out corrupted, depending on whatever value happened to
be on the stack at that point, while version 1 (reached without a
version-search loop running first) happened to decode correctly by
accident. THIS TRANSLATION DOES NOT AND CANNOT REPRODUCE THAT BUG -
Limbo guarantees every declared local starts zero-valued, so there is
no way to write the equivalent of the C's own uninitialized read here.
This translation is therefore unconditionally correct where the real
9front binary is only environment-dependently correct. See
.IR qrtest (1)
and the
.B inferno-rio-qr-encoder-bug
project memory for the full derivation.
.PP
A second, smaller, and much less consequential oddity in the same
source:
.BR evaluate() ,
the scorer used to pick the best of the 8 standard QR mask patterns,
discards the row-scan penalty it just computed
.RB ( "sc = ..." ,
not
.BR "sc += ..." )
before adding the column-scan and block penalties. This one is an
ordinary (not undefined-behaviour) divergence and IS translated
literally, mask-selection quirk included - it appears to only affect
which of the 8 (all individually valid) masks gets picked, not
correctness; see
.IR qrtest (1)
for how this shaped the test suite's own oracle-comparison strategy.
.PP
The real C source's own
.B main()
reads at most one 8192-byte
.BR readn()
from standard input and silently truncates anything longer with no
warning - a real, deliberately preserved limitation of the CLI entry
point specifically (this translation's own
.B run()
test seam has no such limit; it accepts whatever byte array it is
given).
.PP
A third oddity in
.BR evaluate() ,
found the hard way, not by re-reading: its run-length scan sets a
local run counter to 0 on every color change but never increments it
anywhere - checked twice directly against the real source, since this
looks exactly like a missing increment. It is not one: the counter is
provably always 0 at the "5 or more consecutive modules" penalty
check, so that entire scoring rule is DEAD CODE in the real, deployed
source. An early translation pass added the "missing" increment,
assuming an oversight; this changed which of the 8 masks scores best
on many inputs, causing widespread module-content mismatches with
matching side/length (traced with a minimal single-case diff probe,
confirmed with a genuine negative control - reintroducing the
increment reproduces the exact divergence). Left as dead code here,
translated literally, matching the real source.
.PP
Two real TRANSLATION bugs (not source bugs), both found only by
running the test suite, not by re-reading:
.B run()
itself did not defensively reload
.B sys
the way every other test-seam
.B run()
in this backlog does (see
.IR cb (1)'s
own
.BR loadmods() ) -
missing here, it crashed
.I emu
itself (a native fault, misreported by the crash handler as an
unrelated "module not loaded") the first time
.IR qrtest (1)
called
.B run()
directly, since
.I qr.b's
own
.B init()
had never run to load it. And the evaluate() dead-counter mistake
above was itself introduced by this translation, not present in the
source, before being caught and reverted.
